    {
      "id": "d5289d8a223e6ca04c490448671402efc75c1150",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580462683,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "If you're testing whether an index into a C array is within bounds, should the macro take two arguments - the index and the array size - and do the < test itself?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T06:09:30.564Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:25:04.804Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 580508014,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "It introduces some additional complexity that I don't think is warranted. The only reason to do that that I can think of is to have failure messages with numerical values included, but that's not a very compelling reason to me in this context.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T07:25:04.743Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:25:04.743Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4f0cb57ecfc3136cf57a314e39f7cdfbfde3d681",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580465957,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Also, having `ws_assert()` be disabled by `WS_DISABLE_ASSERT` but `ws_assert_bounds()` not be disabled by `WS_DISABLE_ASSERT` might be a bit surprising - we should probably have something in the API names to distinguish between \"check that's disabled by `WS_DISABLE_ASSERT`\" and \"check that's always done\".",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T06:16:50.934Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:32:37.959Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 580514031,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Maybe possibly just a bit surprising, and it's a pretty benign surprise in any case, but do you have a suggestion for a name? Should we use `ws_assert_true()` by analogy with GLib and drop the qualifying specifics from the name?\n\nRegardless of the naming schema one or more unconditional assertions should exist because sometimes it is convenient and maybe even necessary to pacify static analyzers, in my experience.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T07:32:37.905Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:32:37.905Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1c1022e386cc44e106f0073a4be64e46c2de8056",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580515330,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> The only reason to do that that I can think of is to have failure messages with numerical values included, but that's not a very compelling reason to me in this context.\n\nThe name indicates that `ws_assert_bounds()` is intended for array bounds checking, but, if it just takes an arbitrary expression as an argument, what prevents people from just using it as \"ws_assert_but_not_disabled_by_WS_DISABLE_ASSERT()`, with all sorts of tests?\n\nAnd, if the answer is \"nothing, and that's OK\", perhaps it should be renamed to something that indicates that it's for use with arbitrary tests that should be done even in non-WS_DISABLE_ASSERT builds.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T07:34:26.474Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:34:26.474Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "14efeb8a5f5b9e009588c9bd5f0f1638edcf3086",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580520422,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> Maybe possibly just a bit surprising, and it's a pretty benign surprise in any case, but do you have a suggestion for a name?\n\n`ws_abort_if_not()`?\n\n> Should we use `ws_assert_true()` by analogy with GLib\n\nI had to look up the documentation to see what the heck the difference was between `ws_assert()` and `ws_assert_true()`, because `_true` didn't add anything obvious.\n\n`ws_abort_if_not()` indicates the code will abort if the condition is not true, unlike `ws_assert()` which is merely saying \"I'm claiming that this will always be true at this point\" without directly indicating what the consequences of it not being true are (as the consequences depend on whether `WS_DISABLE_ASSERT` is defined, either being \"nothing\" or \"it aborts\").",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T07:40:51.760Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:40:51.760Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ab1e3f7297902b8c483fd92aae75f2ec9ca84b9c",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580530530,
          "type": null,
          "body": ">>>\nThe name indicates that `ws_assert_bounds()` is intended for array bounds checking, but, if it just takes an arbitrary expression as an argument, what prevents people from just using it as \"ws_assert_but_not_disabled_by_WS_DISABLE_ASSERT()\\`, with all sorts of tests?\n>>>\n\nIt's not OK to misuse `ws_assert_bounds()` but there isn't necessarily an enforcement mechanism. The idea is that new aliases to `g_assert_true()` would be added instead as the need arises. The chosen name is supposed to document use-cases for not using an assertion conditional on WS_DISABLE_ASSERT but maybe that is overkill or should use a comment instead.\n\n>>>\nAnd, if the answer is \"nothing, and that's OK\", perhaps it should be renamed to something that indicates that it's for use with arbitrary tests that should be done even in non-WS_DISABLE_ASSERT builds.\n>>>\n\nThat's option 2, drop the categorization from the name. Works for me too.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2021-05-20T07:52:56.915Z",
          "updated_at": "2021-05-20T07:52:56.915Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 100660273,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 3044,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "94eb6a846e094e0c4855e84a8f6ba1830ecb9f0a",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 580596885,
          "type": null,
          "body": ">>>\n`ws_abort_if_not()` indicates the code will abort if the condition is not true, unlike `ws_assert()` which is merely saying \"I'm claiming that this will always be true at this point\" without directly indicating what the consequences of it not being true are (as the consequences depend on whether `WS_DISABLE_ASSERT` is defined, either being \"nothing\" or \"it aborts\").\n>>>\n\nI don't find that distinction to be very meaningful. People understand (correctly IMO) `ws_assert(condition)` to mean the same as `ws_abort_with_message_if_not(condition)`. The fact that we may choose to optimize it away under certain conditions is an implementation detail that doesn't change those semantics.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
